**How to increase the current sensitivity.** From $S_I = NAB/k$:
- increase the number of turns $N$;
- increase the area $A$ of the coil (limited by the size of the instrument);
- increase $B$, using a strong magnet and a soft iron core;
- decrease the torsional constant $k$, using a fine phosphor-bronze spring or suspension.

**A catch with increasing N.** Doubling the number of turns doubles the current sensitivity, but if the same kind of wire is used it also doubles the length of wire and hence the resistance $G$. The voltage sensitivity $S_V = NAB/kG$ then stays the **same**. So increasing the current sensitivity does not necessarily increase the voltage sensitivity. Phosphor bronze is preferred for the spring or suspension because a thin strip of it has a small torsional constant yet high tensile strength, it conducts electricity (so it can lead the current into the coil), and it is not easily fatigued by repeated twisting.

**Why a galvanometer is not used directly as a meter.** A typical galvanometer gives full-scale deflection for a current of a few microamperes to a few milliamperes, and has a resistance of tens to hundreds of ohms. Placed directly in a circuit carrying a larger current it would be damaged, and its own resistance would change the current being measured. Placed across a component to measure a p.d., it would draw a sizeable current and change the p.d. It must therefore be converted, as described below.
